Pleading Under the Federal Rules
The Rules, promulgated in 1938, swept away centuries of doctrine on common law and equity pleading. In the vision of Charles Clark, their principal architect, they were a radical departure not only from common law and equity pleading, but in some respects from code pleading as well. A guiding principle for the Federal Rules’ approach to pleading was to simplify the procedure so that pleading would no longer be the main event in litigation, as it was under the common law, but merely a step to facilitate the disposition of the parties’ dispute based on the merits—the evidence and arguments developed during the course of litigation.



•In 1938, the Federal Rules implemented a new vision of procedure, replacing common law and equity pleading

INTRODUCTORY NOTE ON PLEADING BY PLAINTIFFS UNDER THE RULES
The following Rules form the core of the Rules’ approach to pleading, which revolves around the filing of a complaint by the plaintiff. As you read the Rules, look for answers to the following questions, each of which examines how the Rules revised—or even outright rejected—prior approaches to pleading:

Rule 2: Under the Federal Rules, what has become of the old common law forms of action?

Rules 2 and 3: What has become of the use of writs for pleading? Put another way, how does a plaintiff initiate a civil suit?

Rule 7(a): What has become of pleadings beyond the complaint and answer, such as replications and rejoinders?

Rule 8: What must pleadings contain? How does this differ from the requirements under the common law? How does does this differ from the requirements under the Field Code that a complaint contain “[a] statement of the facts constituting the cause of action” and “[a] demand for relief”?
